1
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:26,380
Hi everyone, I'm Anna, your instructor in this IELTS writing 5.0 to 6.0 course

2
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:27,120
at PrEP.

3
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:31,400
Now that we have covered the basic marking criteria, we are going to look at

4
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,540
the IELTS writing test as a whole.

5
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:34,520
Why?

6
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,500
Because you can have excellent grammar and vocabulary, but if you mismanage

7
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,900
your 60 minutes, you might still walk away with a lower band score than you

8
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:44,240
deserve.

9
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:46,960
This section has three main goals.

10
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,680
First, to understand the structure of the 60 minute test.

11
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,180
Second, to master the value proposition, why task two matters more.

12
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:00,920
And third, to learn to manage time and panic without strict formulas.

13
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,980
Let's step onto the battlefield.

14
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:10,480
The IELTS writing test is a race against the clock.

15
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:14,380
You have exactly one hour to complete two distinct tasks.

16
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,520
Let's look at them side by side.

17
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:21,080
Task one is your data task, where you describe visual information.

18
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:24,400
You need to write at least 150 words.

19
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,540
It requires you to be objective, factual, and concise.

20
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:32,120
Task two is your discursive essay.

21
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:35,460
You need to write at least 250 words.

22
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,800
This requires you to argue, discuss, and support opinions.

23
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:43,320
The examiners do not tell you when to switch tasks.

24
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:47,160
They just say start and 60 minutes later, stop.

25
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,060
How you use that hour is entirely up to you.

26
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:52,600
Here is the most important secret of the test.

27
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:54,860
All tasks are not created equal.

28
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,820
Many students try to split their effort 50-50.

29
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:02,100
They think two tasks, so I should give equal energy to both.

30
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:03,560
This is a mistake.

31
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:06,740
Task two is worth double the marks of task one.

32
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:08,080
Think of it this way.

33
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:13,420
Task two contributes to two thirds of your final writing score, while task one

34
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:14,980
contributes only one third.

35
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:20,560
If you write a perfect task one essay, but you run out of time and write a half

36
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:24,140
-finished essay for task two, your final score will suffer heavily.

37
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:29,460
However, if your task one is just okay, but you pour your energy into a

38
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:33,880
brilliant task two essay, your overall score will be pulled up significantly.

39
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:38,740
This leads us to our first strategic rule, protect task two at all costs.

40
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,140
Now let's talk about time management.

41
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:50,400
You will often hear advice like, spend 20 minutes on task one and 40 minutes on

42
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:51,080
task two.

43
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,580
This is a good guideline, but it is not a law.

44
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:58,900
Instead of a fixed formula, I want you to use a priority principle.

45
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:00,480
The logic is simple.

46
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:04,400
Task two has a much bigger impact on your final score.

47
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:08,340
Because it carries twice the weight, your time is better spent there.

48
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:13,520
Therefore, you should never steal time from task two just to make task one

49
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:13,980
perfect.

50
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:18,700
If task one is taking you 25 minutes, you are now entering the danger zone.

51
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,580
You are stealing time from your main essay.

52
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:27,160
In that situation, it is better to finish task one simply, even if it feels a

53
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:29,940
bit basic, and move immediately to task two.

54
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:33,060
A good enough task one allows you to survive.

55
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,620
A great task two allows you to thrive.

56
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:41,060
Conversely, if you are a fast writer and finish task one in 15 minutes, do not

57
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:41,820
just sit there.

58
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,360
Reinvest that extra five minutes into planning your task two even better.

59
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:50,000
Be flexible, but always prioritise the heavy lifter.

60
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:58,360
Finally, let's address the invisible enemy in the exam room, panic.

61
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:03,080
When we look at the clock and see only 10 minutes left, our brains often go

62
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,020
into fight-or-flight mode.

63
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:08,600
When you panic, you experience cognitive tunnel vision.

64
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:11,080
Your vocabulary range shrinks.

65
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:14,880
You start making simple grammar mistakes you would never make at home.

66
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:18,600
You might write faster, but your handwriting becomes illegible.

67
00:04:19,279 --> 00:04:22,600
Panic is not just a feeling, it is a performance killer.

68
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:24,940
So, what is the strategy?

69
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,980
If you feel panic rising, perhaps you realise you misunderstood the prompt, or

70
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:33,200
you are running out of time, stop writing for 10 seconds.

71
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:38,320
It sounds counterintuitive to stop when you are in a rush, but taking 10

72
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:42,460
seconds to breathe and look at your plan will save you minutes of rambling,

73
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:43,820
incoherent writing.

74
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:47,660
Accept that you cannot write a perfect essay in a panic.

75
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,900
Shift your goal from perfection to completion.

76
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,860
Finish the sentence you are on, write a simple conclusion, check your work.

77
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:00,760
A complete, simple essay is always better than a complex, unfinished mess.

78
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:07,780
Let's wrap up today's strategic overview.

79
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:13,060
We learned that the writing test is 60 minutes of independent time management.

80
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:15,880
We learned the golden rule of waiting.

81
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,300
Task 2 is worth double task 1, so it must always be your priority.

82
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:26,920
We discussed that you shouldn't strictly follow a 20 to 40 minute rule if it

83
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:27,860
hurts your score.

84
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:31,040
Instead, allocate time based on value.

85
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:34,620
And finally, we learned that panic kills performance.

86
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:39,960
When the pressure hits, pause, reset, and focus on finishing.

87
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,720
In the next lessons, we will go back to specific drills to improve your writing

88
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,120
speed so that time management becomes easy.

89
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:48,380
I'll see you there.

